As we have seen, the moving frame provides us with an intrinsic set of vectors, like coordinate axes, that are special to the particular curve traced by a path. 
In contrast, the velocity and acceleration vectors of a path are definitely not intrinsic quantities but depend on the particular parametrization chosen as well as on the shap of the path. 
(The speed of a paht is entirely independent of the geometry of the curve traced.)
We can get some feeling for the relationship between the intrisnsic notion of the moving frame and the extrinsic quantities of velocity and acceleration by expression the latter two vector functions in terms of the moving frame vectors. 

